          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %strong Geometric function theory
            considers notions of higher generalized functions on higher generalized spaces (such as on
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoid"} orbifolds
            and more generally on
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-stack+homotopically"} infinity-stacks
            = succeed "-structures" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %em pull-tensor-push
            operation in the given
            = succeed "-context." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %h3#motivating_toy_example_matrices Motivating toy example: matrices
          %p
            The motivating toy example is that where all spaces in question are just
            %em finite sets
            and functions are just maps from finite sets to a given
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+field"} ground field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a finite set the collection of functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(X)
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is canonically identified with the vector space spanned by the elements of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_2
            functions on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1 \times X_2
            are canonically identified with
            = succeed "-matrices" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |X_1| \times |X_2|
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Mat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ,
                  %mi m
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  C(X_1 \times X_2) \simeq Mat(n= |X_1|, m = |X_2|)
                  \\,.
          %p
            The point is that from this perspective on matrices the action of a matrix on a vector is given by the pull-tensor-push operation of functions through a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"} span
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ×
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mn 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi pr
                                %mn 1
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ↘
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi pr
                                %mn 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mn 1
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; X_1 \times X_2
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}^{pr_1}\swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow^{pr_2}
                  \\\
                  X_1
                  \&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;
                  X_2
                  }
          %p
            Given
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \in C(X_1)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \in C(X_1 \times X_2)
            the matrix product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi v
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \cdot v \in C(X_2)
            can be understood as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi v
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi pr
                        %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi pr
                    %mn 1
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  M \cdot v =  \int_{pr_2}(M \cdot (pr_1^* (v)))
                  \\,,
          %p where
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi pr
                        %mn 1
                        %mo *
                      %mo :
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ×
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pr_1^* : C(X_1) \to C(X_1 \times X_2)
                is the pullback of functions along the projection map
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi pr
                          %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pr_1
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \cdot (\cdot)
                is the ordinary product of functions;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∫
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi pr
                            %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_{pr_2}
                is the push-forward of field-valued functions along
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi pr
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pr_2
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ×
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1 \times X_2
                over
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1
                to a function on
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_2
          %h3#groupoidification Groupoidification
          %p
            A closely related situation is considered in the context of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoidification"} groupoidification
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoidification"} groupoidification
            functions with values in fields are essentially replaced by functions with values in (finite)
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+cardinality"} groupoid cardinality
            is used to interpret such a gadget as a function with values in rational numbers.
          %p
            More precisely,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoidification"} groupoidification
            in the sense of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"} John Baez
            can be understood as geometric function theory for the case that collections of geometric functions are modeled as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/over+category"} over categories
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/examples+for+geometric+function+objects"} examples for geometric function objects
          %p
            Related toy examples of the general pattern are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            algebras regarded as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+algebra"} category algebras
            (see the end of that entry for the span-description of category algebras).
          %h3#general_categorical__homotopical_setup
            General
            = succeed "-categorical" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            In full generality the idea of
            %em geometric function theory
            is to replace in the above toy example all finite sets with generalized higher spaces in the form of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stack"} ∞-stack
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(X)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(X)
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} monoidal (∞,1)-category
            in a suitable sense and behaves as expected under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullback"} homotopy pullback
            and push-forward operations.
          %p
            The
            %strong main two conditions
            that one wants to have in
            %em geometric function theory
            are
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                      %mo ←
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1 \to Y \leftarrow X_2
                two generalized spaces sitting over a third one, we have an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
                between the generalized functions on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} fiber product
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mo ×
                        %mi Y
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1 \times_Y X_2
                and the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
                of functions on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1
                with functions on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_2
                over functions on
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mo ×
                        %mi Y
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msub
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mrow
                          %mi C
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      C(X_1 \times_Y X_2)
                      \\simeq
                      C(X_1) \otimes_{C(Y)} C(X_2)
                      \\,,
              %p
                where all operations are in the suitable
                = succeed "-context" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limit"} homotopy limit
            %li
              %p
                again for given
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                      %mo ←
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1 \to Y \leftarrow X_2
                the generalized functions on the (homotopy/
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                \-)fiber product are supposed to induce via (homotopy/
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                \-) pull-tensor-push operation through the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"} span
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ←
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mo ×
                        %mi Y
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 2
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1 \leftarrow X_1 \times_{Y} X_2 \to X_2
                all
                = succeed "-linear" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(Y)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(X_1)
                and
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(X_2)
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mo ×
                        %mi Y
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≃
                      %msub
                        %mi Maps
                        %mrow
                          %mi C
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      C(X_1 \times_Y X_2)
                      \\simeq
                      Maps_{C(Y)}(C(X_1), C(X_2))
                      \\,.
          %p
            This is to be read as a vast generalization of ordinary matrix multiplication, to which it reduces in the case that all spaces here are finite sets and all functions are ordinary functions on finite sets with values in some field. One step higher this gives
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fourier-Mukai+transformation"} Fourier-Mukai transformations
            etc.
          %h3#relation_to_hochschild_cohomology Relation to Hochschild (co)homology
          %p A particularly interesting application of items 1) and 2) above is to the case where the pullback diagram of generalized spaces in question is simply
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi Id
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Id
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mrow
                      %mi Id
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Id
                  %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X \stackrel{Id \times Id}{\to}
                  X \times X
                  \\stackrel{Id \times Id}{\leftarrow}
                  X
                  \\,.
          %p
            As discussed at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span+trace"} span trace
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullback"} homotopy pullback
            of this is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space+object"} loop space object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Λ
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda X
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p So in this case statement 2) relates
          %ul
            %li
              generalized functions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Λ
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(\Lambda X)
              on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space"} loop space
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p with
          %ul
            %li
              the homotopy tensor product of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(X)
              with itself over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(X)\otimes C(X)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Λ
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  C(\Lambda X) \simeq C(X) \otimes_{C(X) \otimes C(X)} C(X)
                  \\,.
          %p
            The expression on the right is known as the higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trace"} trace
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} monoidal (∞,1)-category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(X)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild+homology"} Hochschild homology
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(X)
          %h4#realization_in_terms_of_derived_stacks_on_the_algebraic_site
            Realization in terms of derived
            = succeed "-stacks" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            Aspects of geometric function theory have a long history in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaves
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stacks
            over algebraic
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fourier-Mukai+transformation"} Fourier-Mukai transform
            is a classical example of a pull-tensor-push operation in a context where the object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(X)
            of generalized functions on a space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is taken to be
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(X)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+sheaf"} coherent sheaves
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            A general proof of the above item 2 in the context of generalized functions modeled by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(X)
            has been given in
          %ul
            %li
              B. Toën and G. Vezzosi,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Homotopical Geometry I: Topos theory.
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0207028"} arXiv
          %p
            As any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(X)
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+sheaf"} coherent sheaves
            is naturally understood as the
            = succeed "-categorical" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            = succeed "." do
